lease; legacy RPC 2/3/4 dual-stack obligations apply only to explicitly legacy installations and never require a Config-4 endpoint to accept legacy — no byte sniffing, extension field, argv value, environment value or failed handshake can select a fallback; Section 6.6): a negotiator that, given the local installation's configuration generation and the peer's hello, selects exactly one common major or refuses no-common-major with the pinned Section 15 class (incompatible_protocol, exit 6, only when incompatibility is actually known), preserves core-only peers (RPC 2 core sync, unsupported directory/backend activation exposed explicitly as directory_mesh_unsupported / the RPC 4 equivalent, never as zero inventory or a capability claim), never downgrades a Config-4 host-channel endpoint below RPC 5 and never substitutes negotiation for the hostchannel admission (identity equality, allowlist, generation) that TASK-260830-z1yxg9 owns; for explicitly legacy installations select the highest common major among 2/3/4 with the exact per-major contract keys (RPC 2: 14 contracts/6 namespaces; RPC 3: 24 keys/7 namespaces; RPC 4: 25 keys/8 namespaces; RPC 5: RPC 4 shapes with protocol_version 5.0.0) and the historically bound Structured Error version per major; refuse a v2 bound inside a v3+ frame and any cross-major mixing. Tests: drive the negotiator with every pair (local majors × peer hello majors) as a table derived from the pinned text, positives for each admitted selection, negatives for no-common-major, mixed-major frames, a Config-4 endpoint offered legacy, a legacy installation offered RPC 5 only, sniffing/fallback attempts (byte sniffing, extension field, argv, env, failed handshake), and the core-only exposure invariant (a core-only peer's inventory is reported as unsupported activation, not empty); every gate a narrowing mutant; the peer-visible outcomes must be pure data (no capability advertised).\n2|COMMON CONTRACT (first leaf of a Story on trunk 7efe385 or later