{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c5fb","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b01d-7842-7623-9b92-35d8899ee7fd"},"sequence":344,"recorded_at":1780531400000683,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"0ef4e50a-8dbf-492d-ac24-c7f49d0e006b","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"0ef4e50a-8dbf-492d-ac24-c7f49d0e006b","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"0ef4e50a-8dbf-492d-ac24-c7f49d0e006b"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0b01e-446f-7c20-8ea1-e13e1f265b14"},"task_id":"01a0b01e-446f-7c20-8ea1-e13e1f265b14","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0b01e-446f-7c20-8ea1-e13e1f265b14","chunk":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md`.\n(2635 line(s) above)\n2636|### 6.6 Configuration 4.0.0 host-channel migration\n2637|\n2638|Configuration 4.0.0 retains Configuration 3.0.0 except for the following exact\n2639|changes. All other closed members, path precedence and five environment\n2640|variables remain unchanged. No secret or TLS-verification override is added.\n2641|\n2642|| Key | Required value / constraint |\n2643|| --- | --- |\n2644|| <code>schema_version</code> | Exact <code>4.0.0</code> |\n2645|| <code>mesh.transport</code> | Required, exact <code>ssh_tls13</code>; no default or alternative |\n2646|| <code>mesh.host_channel</code> | Required, exact closed table below |\n2647|\n2648|The <code>mesh.host_channel</code> table contains exactly:\n2649|\n2650|| Key | Required value / constraint |\n2651|| --- | --- |\n2652|| <code>version</code> | Exact <code>1.0.0</code> |\n2653|| <code>credential_id</code> | SHA-256 digest of the local leaf certificate DER |\n2654|\n2655|No per-peer override exists: every peer in a Config-4 mesh requires Host\n2656|Channel 1 and RPC 5. The credential is loaded from the machine-local Section\n2657|11.10.2 store; neither a certificate nor private-key bytes belong in TOML.\n2658|The existing endpoint/SSH authentication and host-ID allowlist remain necessary\n2659|but are insufficient without enrolled TLS identity. Unknown/missing/partial\n2660|configuration, credential or trust state is a refusal, never legacy selection.\n2661|\n2662|Migration is an explicit local operator action, never first-connect behavior.\n2663|First upgrade both binaries while keeping the old configuration bytes intact;\n2664|create local credentials and exchange public enrollment material out of band;\n2665|validate all intended peers' UUIDs and fingerprints; then preview a Config-4\n2666|replacement with the retained Config-3 fields, selected credential and complete\n2667|peer set. Apply only after explicit operator confirmation, an owner-only backup,\n2668|and validation of that exact preview against the current configuration/trust\n2669|generation. Older Config-1/2 inputs first use the existing explicit migrations.\n2670|A missing peer enrollment blocks activation; an operator may explicitly remove\n2671|a peer from the preview, but the implementation cannot silently drop it.\n2672|\n2673|Replacement is atomic and crash durable. On restart select only the last fully\n2674|committed configuration plus valid local trust state; partial writes park with\n2675|<code>invalid_config</code>. Config-4 readers MUST NOT rewrite historical files,\n2676|and old readers MUST refuse Config-4 mutation. A Config-4 installation refuses\n2677|the unflagged legacy server invocation before reading stdin. A Config-1/2/3\n2678|installation refuses the flagged host-channel invocation. No byte sniffing,\n2679|extension field, argv host UUID, environment value, or failed handshake can\n2680|select a fallback. Legacy RPC 2/3/4 dual-stack obligations apply only to explicitly\n2681|legacy installations; they never require a Config-4 endpoint to accept legacy.\n2682|An operator rollback requires stopping every host channel, explicit replacement\n2683|of configuration from the preserved backup, and acknowledgement that the legacy\n2684|installation has no Host Channel assurance; it is not a connection retry.\n2685|\n2686|## 7. Provider plugin protocol\n2687|\n2688|### 7.1 Discovery and trust\n2689|\n2690|Built-in support covers <code>codex</code>, <code>claude</code>,\n2691|<code>gemini</code>, <code>muse</code>, <code>antigravity</code>, and\n2692|<code>pi</code>. External provider executables are named\n2693|<code>ax-provider-&lt;id&gt;</code>, where <code>id</code> matches\n2694|<code>[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,31}</code>.\n2695|\n2696|Candidate discovery order is:\n2697|\n2698|1. configured <code>providers.plugin_dirs</code> in listed order;\n2699|2. built-in adapters; then\n2700|3. <code>PATH</code>, only when <code>allow_path_plugins</code> is true.\n2701|\n2702|M0 establishes the versioned plugin wire contracts, internal implementation\n2703|interfaces, and a conformance harness. M0 MUST NOT advertise a public stable\n2704|plugin SDK. A stable public SDK remains deferred until both Codex and Claude\n2705|implementations validate that the boundary is sufficient without provider-\n2706|specific authority leakage or compatibility shims.\n2707|\n2708|The order does not establish precedence. If two candidates—including a built-in\n2709|and an executable—declare the same provider ID, discovery MUST fail with\n2710|<code>invalid_config</code> before either candidate is probed or executed.\n2711|Configuration schema 1.0.0 has no duplicate-selection override; the operator\n2712|must remove or rename one candidate. For each accepted external candidate, the\n2713|canonical absolute executable path and SHA-256 digest MUST be recorded at trust\n2714|time. Symlinks MUST be resolved before comparison, the target MUST be a regular\n2715|file owned by the operator or an administrator-approved identity, and a changed\n2716|path target or digest MUST require renewed trust. Plugins execute with the\n2717|operator's privileges and are inside the trusted-host boundary, but they MUST\n2718|receive only the minimum operation-specific inputs.\n2719|\n2720|### 7.2 Framing and lifecycle\n2721|\n2722|The provider protocol is line-delimited JSON over stdin/stdout with\n2723|<code>protocol = \"urn:ax:protocol:provider\"</code> and\n2724|<code>protocol_version = \"2.0.0\"</code>. Each line MUST be one complete UTF-8\n2725|JSON object no larger than 8 MiB. Stdout MUST contain protocol frames only;\n2726|human diagnostics go to stderr.\n2727|\n2728|<code>ax</code> starts one plugin process per operation. Requests are\n2729|single-flight in v2. After one response, <code>ax</code> closes stdin and the\n2730|plugin MUST exit. Exit 0 requires a successful response. A structured error\n2731|response SHOULD exit 0 because the protocol succeeded; a crash, invalid frame,\n2732|or missing response is a provider-host failure.\n2733|\n2734|Request envelope:\n2735|\n2736|~~~json\n2737|{\n2738|  \"protocol\": \"urn:ax:protocol:provider\",\n2739|  \"protocol_version\": \"2.0.0\",\n2740|  \"request_id\": \"0198f4c8-8e50-7f66-8f70-1234567890ab\",\n2741|  \"operation\": \"doctor\",\n2742|  \"deadline\": \"2026-08-19T04:05:00.000Z\",\n2743|  \"body\": {\n2744|    \"platform\": \"macos\",\n2745|    \"architecture\": \"arm64\",\n2746|    \"provider_executable\": \"/opt/local/bin/pi\",\n2747|    \"identity\": null\n2748|  }\n2749|}\n2750|~~~\n2751|\n2752|The request envelope contains exactly <code>protocol</code>,\n2753|<code>protocol_version</code>, <code>request_id</code> UUIDv7,\n2754|<code>operation</code> from the Section 7.5 operation registry,\n2755|<code>deadline</code> timestamp, and the operation-specific <code>body</code>.\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
