{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c603","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b01d-7842-7623-9b92-35d8899ee7fd"},"sequence":348,"recorded_at":1780531400000691,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"0ef4e50a-8dbf-492d-ac24-c7f49d0e006b","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"0ef4e50a-8dbf-492d-ac24-c7f49d0e006b","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"0ef4e50a-8dbf-492d-ac24-c7f49d0e006b"},"call_id":"call_01a0b01e3e10715e91efda787401b6f1","text":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md`.\n(2635 line(s) above)\n2636|### 6.6 Configuration 4.0.0 host-channel migration\n2637|\n2638|Configuration 4.0.0 retains Configuration 3.0.0 except for the following exact\n2639|changes. All other closed members, path precedence and five environment\n2640|variables remain unchanged. No secret or TLS-verification override is added.\n2641|\n2642|| Key | Required value / constraint |\n2643|| --- | --- |\n2644|| <code>schema_version</code> | Exact <code>4.0.0</code> |\n2645|| <code>mesh.transport</code> | Required, exact <code>ssh_tls13</code>; no default or alternative |\n2646|| <code>mesh.host_channel</code> | Required, exact closed table below |\n2647|\n2648|The <code>mesh.host_channel</code> table contains exactly:\n2649|\n2650|| Key | Required value / constraint |\n2651|| --- | --- |\n2652|| <code>version</code> | Exact <code>1.0.0</code> |\n2653|| <code>credential_id</code> | SHA-256 digest of the local leaf certificate DER |\n2654|\n2655|No per-peer override exists: every peer in a Config-4 mesh requires Host\n2656|Channel 1 and RPC 5. The credential is loaded from the machine-local Section\n2657|11.10.2 store; neither a certificate nor private-key bytes belong in TOML.\n2658|The existing endpoint/SSH authentication and host-ID allowlist remain necessary\n2659|but are insufficient without enrolled TLS identity. Unknown/missing/partial\n2660|configuration, credential or trust state is a refusal, never legacy selection.\n2661|\n2662|Migration is an explicit local operator action, never first-connect behavior.\n2663|First upgrade both binaries while keeping the old configuration bytes intact;\n2664|create local credentials and exchange public enrollment material out of band;\n2665|validate all intended peers' UUIDs and fingerprints; then preview a Config-4\n2666|replacement with the retained Config-3 fields, selected credential and complete\n2667|peer set. Apply only after explicit operator confirmation, an owner-only backup,\n2668|and validation of that exact preview against the current configuration/trust\n2669|generation. Older Config-1/2 inputs first use the existing explicit migrations.\n2670|A missing peer enrollment blocks activation; an operator may explicitly remove\n2671|a peer from the preview, but the implementation cannot silently drop it.\n2672|\n2673|Replacement is atomic and crash durable. On restart select only the last fully\n2674|committed configuration plus valid local trust state; partial writes park with\n2675|<code>invalid_config</code>. Config-4 readers MUST NOT rewrite historical files,\n2676|and old readers MUST refuse Config-4 mutation. A Config-4 installation refuses\n2677|the unflagged legacy server invocation before reading stdin. A Config-1/2/3\n2678|installation refuses the flagged host-channel invocation. No byte sniffing,\n2679|extension field, argv host UUID, environment value, or failed handshake can\n2680|select a fallback. Legacy RPC 2/3/4 dual-stack obligations apply only to explicitly\n2681|legacy installations; they never require a Config-4 endpoint to accept legacy.\n2682|An operator rollback requires stopping every host channel, explicit replacement\n2683|of configuration from the preserved backup, and acknowledgement that the legacy\n2684|installation has no Host Channel assurance; it is not a connection retry.\n2685|\n2686|## 7. Provider plugin protocol\n2687|\n2688|### 7.1 Discovery and trust\n2689|\n2690|Built-in support covers <code>codex</code>, <code>claude</code>,\n2691|<code>gemini</code>, <code>muse</code>, <code>antigravity</code>, and\n2692|<code>pi</code>. External provider executables are named\n2693|<code>ax-provider-&lt;id&gt;</code>, where <code>id</code> matches\n2694|<code>[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,31}</code>.\n2695|\n2696|Candidate discovery order is:\n2697|\n2698|1. configured <code>providers.plugin_dirs</code> in listed order;\n2699|2. built-in adapters; then\n2700|3. <code>PATH</code>, only when <code>allow_path_plugins</code> is true.\n2701|\n2702|M0 establishes the versioned plugin wire contracts, internal implementation\n2703|interfaces, and a conformance harness. M0 MUST NOT advertise a public stable\n2704|plugin SDK. A stable public SDK remains deferred until both Codex and Claude\n2705|implementations validate that the boundary is sufficient without provider-\n2706|specific authority leakage or compatibility shims.\n2707|\n2708|The order does not establish precedence. If two candidates—including a built-in\n2709|and an executable—declare the same provider ID, discovery MUST fail with\n2710|<code>invalid_config</code> before either candidate is probed or executed.\n2711|Configuration schema 1.0.0 has no duplicate-selection override; the operator\n2712|must remove or rename one candidate. For each accepted external candidate, the\n2713|canonical absolute executable path and SHA-256 digest MUST be recorded at trust\n2714|time. Symlinks MUST be resolved before comparison, the target MUST be a regular\n2715|file owned by the operator or an administrator-approved identity, and a changed\n2716|path target or digest MUST require renewed trust. Plugins execute with the\n2717|operator's privileges and are inside the trusted-host boundary, but they MUST\n2718|receive only the minimum operation-specific inputs.\n2719|\n2720|### 7.2 Framing and lifecycle\n2721|\n2722|The provider protocol is line-delimited JSON over stdin/stdout with\n2723|<code>protocol = \"urn:ax:protocol:provider\"</code> and\n2724|<code>protocol_version = \"2.0.0\"</code>. Each line MUST be one complete UTF-8\n2725|JSON object no larger than 8 MiB. Stdout MUST contain protocol frames only;\n2726|human diagnostics go to stderr.\n2727|\n2728|<code>ax</code> starts one plugin process per operation. Requests are\n2729|single-flight in v2. After one response, <code>ax</code> closes stdin and the\n2730|plugin MUST exit. Exit 0 requires a successful response. A structured error\n2731|response SHOULD exit 0 because the protocol succeeded; a crash, invalid frame,\n2732|or missing response is a provider-host failure.\n2733|\n2734|Request envelope:\n2735|\n2736|~~~json\n2737|{\n2738|  \"protocol\": \"urn:ax:protocol:provider\",\n2739|  \"protocol_version\": \"2.0.0\",\n2740|  \"request_id\": \"0198f4c8-8e50-7f66-8f70-1234567890ab\",\n2741|  \"operation\": \"doctor\",\n2742|  \"deadline\": \"2026-08-19T04:05:00.000Z\",\n2743|  \"body\": {\n2744|    \"platform\": \"macos\",\n2745|    \"architecture\": \"arm64\",\n2746|    \"provider_executable\": \"/opt/local/bin/pi\",\n2747|    \"identity\": null\n2748|  }\n2749|}\n2750|~~~\n2751|\n2752|The request envelope contains exactly <code>protocol</code>,\n2753|<code>protocol_version</code>, <code>request_id</code> UUIDv7,\n2754|<code>operation</code> from the Section 7.5 operation registry,\n2755|<code>deadline</code> timestamp, and the operation-specific <code>body</code>.\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c605","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b01d-7842-7623-9b92-35d8899ee7fd"},"sequence":349,"recorded_at":1780531400000693,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"0ef4e50a-8dbf-492d-ac24-c7f49d0e006b","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"0ef4e50a-8dbf-492d-ac24-c7f49d0e006b","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"0ef4e50a-8dbf-492d-ac24-c7f49d0e006b"},"call_id":"call_01a0b01e40f573858a7ab00f526dd83b","text":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md`.\n(14700 line(s) above)\n14701|## 15. Errors and exit semantics\n14702|\n14703|### 15.1 Structured Error\n14704|\n14705|All machine-readable errors use <code>urn:ax:schema:error</code>. Existing\n14706|Provider/RPC/Bridge and legacy CLI surfaces select version <code>1.0.0</code>,\n14707|whose exact shape is:\n14708|\n14709|| Field | Type | Constraint |\n14710|| --- | --- | --- |\n14711|| <code>schema</code> | string | Exact Structured Error schema identifier |\n14712|| <code>schema_version</code> | semver | Exact <code>1.0.0</code> |\n14713|| <code>code</code> | string[1..128] | Stable lower-snake-case registry value |\n14714|| <code>message</code> | string[1..4096] | Human text; automation does not branch on it |\n14715|| <code>exit_code</code> | int32 | Exact Section 15.2 code assigned to <code>code</code> |\n14716|| <code>retryable</code> | boolean | True only when the identical request may safely be retried without new authority or confirmation |\n14717|| <code>operation_id</code> | UUIDv7, optional | Present whenever an operation was allocated |\n14718|| <code>session_id</code> | UUIDv7, optional | Present whenever the failure is session-scoped |\n14719|| <code>details</code> | diagnostic data map | Required, redacted open map constrained below |\n14720|\n14721|~~~json\n14722|{\n14723|  \"schema\": \"urn:ax:schema:error\",\n14724|  \"schema_version\": \"1.0.0\",\n14725|  \"code\": \"workspace_conflict\",\n14726|  \"message\": \"destination differs from its last materialized checkpoint\",\n14727|  \"exit_code\": 5,\n14728|  \"retryable\": false,\n14729|  \"operation_id\": \"0198f4c8-b180-7299-9273-1234567890ab\",\n14730|  \"session_id\": \"0198f4c8-3e70-7a11-8a2b-1234567890ab\",\n14731|  \"details\": {\n14732|    \"expected_checkpoint\": \"sha256:2222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222222\",\n14733|    \"remediations\": [\"diff\", \"copy\", \"worktree\", \"replace_managed_replica\"]\n14734|  }\n14735|}\n14736|~~~\n14737|\n14738|<code>schema</code>, <code>schema_version</code>, <code>code</code>,\n14739|<code>message</code>, <code>exit_code</code>, <code>retryable</code>, and\n14740|<code>details</code> are required. Operation and session IDs are optional and\n14741|MUST be present when known. Messages are for humans; automation MUST branch on\n14742|<code>code</code> and <code>exit_code</code>. Details MUST be redacted and\n14743|schema-valid.\n14744|\n14745|The top-level object is closed. <code>message</code> is 1–4,096 UTF-8\n14746|characters. <code>details</code> is an explicitly open diagnostic data map, not\n14747|an implicit schema extension: it has 0–64 keys matching\n14748|<code>[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,63}</code>, JSON values of maximum nesting depth 4, and\n14749|maximum canonical size 16 KiB. Readers MAY ignore unknown detail keys and MUST\n14750|never infer success, authority, or a remediation action from them. No detail\n14751|may contain a credential, raw transcript, environment secret, or opaque bundle\n14752|content.\n14753|\n14754|Structured Error is independently versioned as a reusable schema, but that\n14755|does not imply independent negotiation inside every envelope. Provider\n14756|protocol <code>2.x</code>, task-board bridge <code>1.x</code>, and Mesh RPC\n14757|<code>2.x</code> each embed exactly Structured Error <code>1.0.0</code>. A\n14758|supported-major failure MUST use that exact object; the containing protocol\n14759|version is sufficient to select it. RPC hello MUST NOT advertise or negotiate\n14760|an <code>error</code> contract key. A future containing-protocol version may\n14761|bind another error version only by stating the new binding explicitly.\n14762|\n14763|Session Adapter 1.0 and <code>session.clone.*</code> bind Structured Error\n14764|1.1.0. It retains the 1.0.0 shape and codes, changes only\n14765|<code>schema_version</code> to exact <code>1.1.0</code>, and adds:\n14766|\n14767|| Exit | Stable clone codes |\n14768|| ---: | --- |\n14769|| 4 | <code>source_not_found</code>, <code>source_ambiguous</code> |\n14770|| 6 | <code>unsupported_environment_tuple</code>, <code>operation_unknown</code>, <code>target_resume_invalid</code> |\n14771|| 9 | <code>source_corrupt</code>, <code>bundle_integrity_failed</code>, <code>credential_material_detected</code> |\n14772|| 11 | <code>source_not_quiescent</code>, <code>source_changed_during_clone</code> |\n14773|| 12 | <code>target_prepare_failed</code>, <code>target_validation_failed</code>, <code>target_checkpoint_failed</code>, <code>transaction_unknown</code> |\n14774|| 13 | <code>session_adapter_protocol_error</code>, <code>session_adapter_process_failed</code>, <code>session_adapter_timeout</code> |\n14775|| 16 | <code>projection_loss_unacceptable</code>, <code>unsafe_pending_action</code> |\n14776|\n14777|Existing semantically identical codes remain reused. Transaction unknown is a\n14778|parked ambiguous effect, never success or absence. Target checkpoint failure\n14779|occurs only after Provider commit and grants no rollback; the exact inert target\n14780|remains recoverable.\n14781|\n14782|Bootstrap and incompatible-major behavior is exact:\n14783|\n14784|| Surface | Supported-major failure before normal success | Unsupported major or unparseable first frame/output |\n14785|| --- | --- | --- |\n14786|| Provider JSON-over-stdio | A syntactically valid v2 request whose operation/body fails receives the Section 7.2 v2 failure envelope with Error 1.0.0 | The plugin MUST NOT masquerade a different-major object as v2. The host accepts no child error object, terminates/waits for exit as applicable, and emits its own local Error 1.0.0: <code>incompatible_protocol</code> for a recognizable major mismatch, otherwise <code>provider_protocol_error</code> |\n14787|| Task-board bridge facade | A syntactically valid v1 invocation failure receives the Section 9.2 v1 failure envelope with Error 1.0.0 | The facade output is not accepted as a bridge result. <code>ax</code> emits its own local Error 1.0.0: <code>incompatible_protocol</code> for a recognizable major mismatch, otherwise <code>task_board_bridge_unavailable</code> |\n14788|| Mesh RPC | Before hello success, a syntactically valid v2 non-hello request or invalid v2 hello receives one v2 failure envelope with Error 1.0.0 and the server then closes | An unsupported protocol major, invalid JSON, oversize line, missing protocol/version/request ID, or response that cannot be framed causes close without an RPC error frame. The initiator emits its own local Error 1.0.0: <code>incompatible_protocol</code> for a recognizable major mismatch, otherwise <code>transport_failure</code> |\n14789|\n14790|A local error above is an <code>ax</code> CLI/observation result, not a forged\n14791|response attributed to the child or peer; it omits unknown operation/session\n14792|IDs and records redacted diagnostics only. Receivers MUST NOT parse a different\n14793|major's payload far enough to trust its error code, retryable bit, details, or\n14794|authority fields.\n14795|\n14796|The normative binding fixtures are\n14797|<code>ERR-PROVIDER-COMPAT</code>, <code>ERR-BRIDGE-COMPAT</code>, and\n14798|<code>ERR-RPC-COMPAT</code>, which use the documented provider/RPC v2 and bridge\n14799|v1 failure envelopes; <code>ERR-PROVIDER-MAJOR</code>,\n14800|<code>ERR-BRIDGE-MAJOR</code>, and <code>ERR-RPC-MAJOR</code>, which change only\n14801|the containing protocol major to 3 for provider/RPC or 2 for the bridge and require the local\n14802|<code>incompatible_protocol</code> result; and\n14803|<code>ERR-PROVIDER-FIRST</code>, <code>ERR-BRIDGE-FIRST</code>, and\n14804|<code>ERR-RPC-FIRST</code>. Provider and RPC fixtures submit valid v2 envelopes\n14805|with an invalid first operation/body and require the bound v2 error; the bridge\n14806|fixture uses its valid v1 invocation and bound v1 error. The RPC fixture submits\n14807|<code>health.get</code> before hello, requires one bound v2 error, and then EOF.\n14808|<code>ERR-FIRST-UNFRAMED</code> supplies invalid JSON to\n14809|each stdio parser (or invalid JSON output from the bridge) and requires no\n14810|remote/child error to be trusted.\n14811|\n14812|### 15.2 Exit codes\n14813|\n14814|| Exit | Meaning |\n14815|| ---: | --- |\n14816|| 0 | Requested operation succeeded |\n14817|| 2 | Usage, invalid flag, or interactive choice required |\n14818|| 3 | Configuration or local precondition invalid |\n14819|| 4 | Session, checkpoint, peer, provider, or task-board identity not found |\n14820|| 5 | Workspace/native-store conflict; no silent overwrite |\n14821|| 6 | Capability unsupported, unknown, conditional, or incompatible |\n14822|| 7 | Authentication/authorization/allowlist failure |\n14823|| 8 | SSH/RPC transport failure; staging can be resumable |\n14824|| 9 | Integrity, schema, hash, or unsafe-path failure |\n14825|| 10 | Ownership/lease/fencing failure |\n14826|| 11 | Busy, quiesce timeout, or graceful stop timeout |\n14827|| 12 | Staging/materialization/rollback failure |\n14828|| 13 | Provider plugin/process failure |\n14829|| 14 | Task-board bridge/bundle/validation failure |\n14830|| 15 | Partial success: immutable sync succeeded but a requested materialization or peer did not |\n14831|| 16 | Explicit policy refusal, including missing destructive confirmation |\n14832|| 17 | Contract/schema migration required |\n14833|| 130 | Interrupted by operator signal before a clean response; inspect authority before retry |\n14834|\n14835|### 15.3 Stable error codes\n14836|\n14837|Protocol 1.0.0 fixes these code-to-exit mappings:\n14838|\n14839|| Exit | Stable error codes |\n14840|| ---: | --- |\n14841|| 2 | <code>interactive_choice_required</code>, <code>invalid_arguments</code> |\n14842|| 3 | <code>invalid_config</code>, <code>idempotency_mismatch</code>, <code>local_precondition_failed</code> |\n14843|| 4 | <code>name_ambiguous</code>, <code>not_found</code> |\n14844|| 5 | <code>workspace_conflict</code>, <code>native_store_conflict</code> |\n14845|| 6 | <code>capability_unavailable</code>, <code>profile_mapping_unavailable</code>, <code>incompatible_protocol</code>, <code>incompatible_schema</code>, <code>provider_fork_unsupported</code>, <code>unsupported_backend_identity</code> |\n14846|| 7 | <code>authentication_failed</code>, <code>peer_not_allowlisted</code>, <code>host_identity_mismatch</code> |\n14847|| 8 | <code>owner_unreachable</code>, <code>transport_failure</code> |\n14848|| 9 | <code>unsafe_path</code>, <code>integrity_failure</code> |\n14849|| 10 | <code>not_owner</code>, <code>stale_owner</code>, <code>lease_conflict</code>, <code>invalid_state_transition</code> |\n14850|| 11 | <code>quiesce_timeout</code>, <code>stop_timeout</code>, <code>workspace_group_busy</code>, <code>workspace_group_changed</code> |\n14851|| 12 | <code>staging_incomplete</code>, <code>atomic_commit_unavailable</code>, <code>materialization_failed</code>, <code>rollback_failed</code> |\n14852|| 13 | <code>provider_timeout</code>, <code>provider_protocol_error</code>, <code>provider_process_failed</code> |\n14853|| 14 | <code>task_board_bridge_unavailable</code>, <code>task_board_bundle_invalid</code>, <code>task_board_validate_failed</code>, <code>checkpoint_unavailable</code> |\n14854|| 15 | <code>partial_sync</code> |\n14855|| 16 | <code>confirmation_required</code>, <code>policy_refused</code>, <code>secret_policy_violation</code> |\n14856|| 17 | <code>migration_required</code> |\n14857|| 130 | <code>interrupted</code> |\n14858|\n14859|New error codes MAY be added in a compatible minor contract version, but an\n14860|unknown code retains the envelope's exit class and MUST NOT be interpreted as\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
